.\" tuatara-init(5): the parts between @ signs are made by frontends/tui/man-pages.lisp.
.TH TUATARA-INIT 5 "" "Tuatara @VERSION@" "File Formats"
.ad l
.SH NAME
tuatara\-init \- the init file of tuatara and the folders it keeps
.SH DESCRIPTION
Tuatara reads one file of Lisp when it starts, after its defaults are
installed: the init file.
It is the place for settings, keys of your own and the packages to load.
An error in it is shown on the echo row and the editor starts anyway; the
forms read before the error are kept.
.SS Where it is
.I ~/.config/tuatara/init.lisp
or, when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set,
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tuatara/init.lisp .
.B tuatara \-c
.I FILE
reads another;
.B tuatara \-Q
reads none, and no package is loaded.
.PP
The first time tuatara starts without an init file it writes a starter there,
and never writes the file again: it is yours.
.B tuatara \-\-generate\-config
writes the same starter to
.I generated\-init.lisp
beside it, to compare with or to take new settings from.
The starter lists every setting, each commented out with a paragraph saying
what it does and, after
.BR "Not set:" ,
what it is when it is not set; take away the leading
.B ;;
to use one.
The tabs some languages' own tools require are set there, uncommented.
.SS How it is read
The forms are read in order, in the package
.BR tuatara\-user ,
so that the names below need no prefix.
Every form is run as written; the file is code, so only you should write it.
.SS A setting
A setting is a variable named in asterisks, set with
.BR setf :
.PP
.RS
.nf
(setf *tab\-width* 4)
(setf *dark\-theme* \(aqterminal)
.fi
.RE
.PP
The settings are listed below, each with the first sentence of its
documentation.
In the editor,
.B C\-h v
shows all of it, and what the setting is now.
Settings are kept together by what they are for, in a block of
.BR load\-package ,
after its
.BR :config ,
as the starter does.
.SS Keys
.TP
.BI "(bind\-key " "KEYS COMMAND " ":map " "MAP " :mode " MODE)"
Bind
.I KEYS
(\(aqC\-c t\(aq) to
.IR COMMAND ,
in every buffer, or only where
.B :map
or
.B :mode
say.
.B :map :leader
binds in the leader's map, the keys after the leader key in the modal
layer.
.TP
.BI "(unbind\-key " KEYS ")"
Take a key you or the defaults bound away.
.SS Packages
.TP
.BI "(load\-package " "NAME " :source " SOURCE " :version " VERSION" ...)
Load a package, or set up a part of Tuatara (a group of settings and keys
under a name), the settings and keys after
.BR :config .
It takes
.BR :source ,
.BR :version ,
.BR :file ,
.BR :folder ,
.BR :signers ,
.BR :plain ,
.BR :disabled ,
.BR :if ,
.BR :defer ,
.BR :after ,
.BR :commands ,
.BR :modes ,
.B :init
and
.BR :config .
A package named by a source is fetched in the background the first time,
from a git repository, verified by who signed it and asked about before it is
compiled and loaded.
.TP
.BI "(package\-source " "NAME ADDRESS " :signers " KEYS)"
Name a source of packages: a git repository or a folder of your disk.
.PP
The starter shows each keyword with an example.
.SS Safe defaults
Nothing in the starter changes what tuatara does: every setting is commented
out, so a start with the starter and one without an init file are the same.
A project's language servers and tools run only when you answered that you
trust the project, and a package is compiled and loaded only after you
answered the question about it (\fB*package\-ask\-before\-loading*\fR).
The setting \fB*safe\-directories*\fR names folders whose files are always
opened safely: read only, no mode or hook acting on them.
.B \-Q
starts without the init file and so without any of that, with what is kept
between sessions kept apart for that session.
.SH FOLDERS
Four folders, each named
.IR tuatara/ ,
and where the XDG variable is not set:
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuatara/
.RB ( XDG_CONFIG_HOME )
What you wrote.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tuatara/
.RB ( XDG_STATE_HOME )
What tuatara remembers of what you did.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/tuatara/
.RB ( XDG_DATA_HOME )
What you added to it, and what it fetched.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/tuatara/
.RB ( XDG_CACHE_HOME )
What it makes again when it is gone.
.PP
The settings
.B *state\-directory*
and
.B *cache\-directory*
put the last two elsewhere; a folder named that is not there (a drive not
mounted) is replaced in memory for the session, so that nothing lands on
another drive.
.SH FILES KEPT
A release reads every file an earlier release of its major version wrote, with
the same result.
The files fall in three classes.
.TP
.B Kept
Made by you.
Never lost to a later release.
.TP
.B Remembered
What you did.
Read by every later release; a line it does not understand is never an error,
and for the lists of trust it means the question is asked again, never a
yes.
.TP
.B Rebuilt
May be deleted at any time; tuatara makes it again.
.PP
Every list kept between sessions begins with the line
.BI "# tuatara " "NAME VERSION"
and changes only by adding fields at the end of a line.
@KEPT-FILES@
.PP
The settings, key functions and command line options stay as they are in the
major version; they change only in the next one.
.SH SETTINGS
Each setting, by the part of the starter init file that sets it, with the
first sentence of its documentation.
@SETTINGS@
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tuatara (1)
